const faqItems = [
  { question: "Do I need a medical card to buy cannabis in Maine?", answer: "No. Adults 21 and older can buy adult-use (recreational) cannabis at a licensed Maine dispensary with only a valid government-issued photo ID. No card, certification, or Maine residency is required. A medical route exists separately for qualifying patients who want lower taxes, higher-dose products, or who are under 21." },
  { question: "What is the tax difference between medical and recreational cannabis in Maine?", answer: "Adult-use cannabis is taxed at a 14% state sales-tax rate effective January 1, 2026 (36 M.R.S. §1811, up from 10% under P.L. 2025, ch. 388). Medical cannabis sold through the registered medical program is taxed at Maine's reduced 5.5% state sales-tax rate. For a frequent buyer, that rate gap is the single largest practical difference between the two programs." },
  { question: "Is the medical cannabis program cheaper than buying recreational?", answer: "Usually, on a per-gram tax basis, yes — the 5.5% medical rate is less than half the 14% adult-use rate. But the medical route adds an upfront cost: you must obtain a written certification from a Maine-licensed physician, nurse practitioner, or physician assistant, and providers set their own fees. Occasional buyers may find the certification cost outweighs the tax savings; regular patients usually come out ahead." },
  { question: "Can someone under 21 buy cannabis in Maine?", answer: "Not adult-use cannabis — the adult-use program is strictly 21 and older. A minor may qualify as a medical patient, but only with a provider's written certification and a parent, legal guardian, or person with legal custody serving as the primary caregiver (22 M.R.S. §2425 and §2423-A). Medical cannabis for a minor must be administered in a non-smokeable form." },
  { question: "Does Maine issue a medical marijuana card?", answer: "Not in the ordinary sense. Maine qualifying patients generally use the written certification issued by their medical provider together with government-issued photo ID. The Office of Cannabis Policy states it does not maintain an ordinary patient registry. The Individual Identification Card (IIC) people sometimes call a 'card' is actually an adult-use worker credential, not a patient credential." },
  { question: "Can I use an out-of-state medical card in Maine?", answer: "Sometimes. Maine recognizes patient credentials from jurisdictions on the OCP-approved visiting-patients list — 28 states plus Washington DC as of mid-2026. A visiting patient must carry the home-state credential and photo ID from that same state. Adult-use purchases need no card at all: any adult 21+ with valid photo ID may buy." },
  { question: "Do medical patients get higher possession or grow limits?", answer: "A Maine medical patient's personal cultivation cap is the same numeric limit as adult use — six mature plants, 12 immature plants, and unlimited seedlings (22 M.R.S. §2423-A). The medical advantage is access: a patient may designate a registered caregiver, who may cultivate up to 30 mature plants (or 500 square feet of canopy) on the patient's behalf. Medical dispensaries may also carry higher-dose products than the 200 mg-per-package adult-use edible ceiling." },
  { question: "Can I switch between medical and recreational?", answer: "Yes. The two programs are separate, and nothing stops a qualifying patient from also shopping adult-use stores, or an adult-use buyer from later obtaining a medical certification. Many Maine residents use adult-use stores for convenience and the medical program for the lower tax rate and higher-dose products." }
];
